The parameter of such a coercion has no value: its body computes the type's
value from the four numbers, with no method invocation.
Each of the four, used as a value in the body, is converted where it stands, as
a numeral is: into the type that its place fixes, from its exact value; where
nothing fixes a type, it is an integer of type \EXP{\mathbb{Z}}
(\secref{natparams}).
A number that does not fit the type that its place fixes, at a numeral's four
numbers, is a static error at that numeral's place, as a numeral out of the
range of its place's type is.
So \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}'s coercion is
\EXP{\KWD{coerce}\llbracket\KWD{nat} n,\KWD{nat} r,\KWD{nat} v\rrbracket(x\COLON \TYP{NaturalNumeral}\llbracket{}n,r,v\rrbracket) = v},
and a byte type \EXP{\KWD{value}\;\KWD{object}\;\TYP{Byte}(b\COLON\mathbb{Z}32)}
declares
\EXP{\KWD{coerce}\llbracket\KWD{nat} n,\KWD{nat} r,\KWD{nat} v\rrbracket(x\COLON \TYP{NaturalNumeral}\llbracket{}n,r,v\rrbracket)\;\KWD{where}\;\{v < 256\} = \TYP{Byte}(v)}.
\revision{revival-numeral-numbers}{The revival's text of 9~November 2026 read
the four numbers in a coercion's body as values of type \EXP{\mathbb{Z}}
wherever they stand, so that a coercion converted each by a method, as
\EXP{v.\VAR{asZZ32}}.
The revival decided on 23~November 2026 that each is converted where it stands,
as a numeral is, and is a \EXP{\mathbb{Z}} only where nothing fixes a type.}

